{
  "image_name": "0f0c6a81636d122855de4855eac3ff1e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how lysosomal damage can trigger several regulated cell-death pathways. It centers on a ruptured lysosome releasing contents such as cathepsins and iron into the cytosol after insults like photodamage, detergents, cationic amphiphilic drugs, or other lysosomotropic agents. A key early event is ROS generation: oxidative damage causes protein and lipid peroxidation, while iron and hydrogen peroxide further amplify ROS, promoting ferroptosis.\n\nLysosomal injury also connects to mitochondrial apoptosis. Cathepsin activity appears to activate BID/tBID and influence BAX/BAK at the mitochondrion, overcoming anti-apoptotic BCL-2 family proteins. This leads to cytochrome c release, apoptosome formation with APAF1 and caspase-9, then caspase-3/7 activation and apoptosis.\n\nAt the same time, leaked lysosomal factors seem to promote inflammasome signaling. NLRP3 recruits pro-caspase-1, producing active caspase-1, which processes IL-1β and IL-18 and cleaves gasdermin D. The gasdermin N-terminal fragment forms membrane pores, driving pyroptosis.\n\nA separate branch links lysosomal damage to necroptosis: cathepsins and/or caspase-8 regulation feed into the RIP1–RIP3–MLKL necrosome. Overall, the image emphasizes lysosomal membrane damage as a central hub that can route cells into apoptosis, pyroptosis, necroptosis, or ferroptosis depending on downstream signaling.",
